+## Resolution Operation
+
+**Resolve competing revisions** — `POST /visits/{visit_id}/resolve`
+
+Request:
+```json
+{
+  "selected_revision_id": 6,
+  "coordinator_id": "..."
+}
+```
+
+Marks the selected revision as current and records the coordinator's identity with the resolution. Rejected revisions remain in submission history but are excluded from current state and exports. A resolution can be reversed by a subsequent coordinator action if needed.
+
+## Idempotency
+
+When a submission arrives with a previously accepted idempotency key:
+- If payload matches the original exactly, return the original acceptance result (revision ID and state)
+- If payload differs, reject with HTTP 409 Conflict
+
+The idempotency key is scoped to the submitting worker and visit pair.
+
+## Downstream Accommodations
+
+The whole-visit revision approach shifts complexity from the record store to the tablet and review interfaces. Both downstream nodes must accommodate this choice.
+
+### Tablet-Sync Obligations
+
+**Local revision tracking**: Tablet storage must persist the `base_revision_id` for each visit alongside the draft. When a worker opens a saved draft, the base revision tracks which server state the draft extends. A draft created offline has `base_revision_id: null`; after first submission it carries the assigned revision ID.
+
+**Unresolved-state handling**: When sync receives `state=unresolved` in the response, this is not a failure. The tablet must:
+- Display the competing revisions to the field worker (likely as "Your edit is one of N competing versions awaiting coordinator review")
+- Retain the local draft with its base revision so a worker can submit further corrections if needed
+- Not block the worker from editing other visits or creating new observations
+
+**Retry state machine**: If network drops mid-submission, retry with the same idempotency key. If the original succeeded but the response was lost, the retry returns the assigned revision ID without creating a duplicate. If both the worker's tablet and a colleague's tablet submit edits from the same base before either receives a response, both will create new revisions and both will eventually see unresolved state.
+
+**Consequences**: Tablet storage grows by one revision ID per visit (negligible). The sync UI must explain unresolved state to field workers in terms they understand. Workers accustomed to "submit and forget" will need to understand that concurrent edits pause a visit's path to export.
+
+### Review-Export Obligations
+
+**Export exclusion**: Visits with `state=unresolved` are not export-eligible. The Friday CSV generation queries for `state IN (resolved, pending)` only. This ensures every exported row has been either single-submission or coordinator-reviewed. The cost is that a visit edited concurrently on Thursday may miss Friday's export even if both revisions are scientifically identical.
+
+**Revision comparison interface**: Coordinators need a side-by-side view of competing revisions showing:
+- Full field values for each competing revision (not diffs, since scientific meaning may require whole-visit context)
+- Author and submission timestamp for each
+- The common base revision they diverged from, if available
+- A selection mechanism to pick the winning revision or create a synthesized one
+
+**Resolution workflow**: Selecting a winner marks one revision as current and records the coordinator's identity. The losing revisions remain in submission history but do not contribute to current state or exports. If a coordinator later realizes they selected the wrong revision, they can re-resolve to a different one; the resolution history preserves the sequence of coordinator actions.
+
+**Unresolved visit visibility**: The review interface must surface unresolved visits proactively. A coordinator should see "12 visits awaiting resolution" before they attempt Friday's export, not discover missing visits after the CSV is generated.
+
+**Consequences**: The review queue grows in proportion to concurrent edits, even when those edits touch unrelated fields. A worker correcting observer names on one tablet while another adds a species note creates review work that field-aware merging would have resolved automatically. Coordinators must budget time for this review; the system trades their labor for preservation of whole-visit scientific intent.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent edit handling | Whole-visit revisions with explicit base tracking | Field-aware three-way merge with structured change deltas | Whole-visit revisions preserve the worker's submitted observation as a scientifically meaningful unit. A worker correcting a species identification and its cover estimate together submits those as a coupled intent; automated field-level merging could combine one worker's species correction with another's unrelated observer-name fix, producing a record no one actually observed. The cost is a larger review queue: even edits to disjoint fields create coordinator work. Field-aware merging would be preferable if the coordinators trusted maintained merge rules to respect scientific relationships, or if review queue volume threatened the Friday export schedule. The current choice prioritizes correctness over review efficiency and avoids maintaining merge rules that would need botanical domain knowledge to get right. |
+| Revision identifier format | Sequential integers per visit | UUID per revision, content hash | Sequential integers provide human-readable ordering for coordinator review ("revision 5 vs. 6" is clearer than comparing UUIDs) and compress well in tablet storage. UUIDs would not add meaningful global uniqueness since visit_id already scopes revisions. Content hashes would enable deduplication but complicate base revision tracking when a worker re-submits identical content from a different base. |
+| Unresolved visit export behavior | Exclude from exports until resolved | Include most recent revision with flag, include all revisions with disambiguation suffix | Excluding unresolved visits from exports ensures the partner receives only coordinator-reviewed data and makes export correctness inspectable: every row came from either a single submission or an explicit coordinator choice. Including the most recent revision would meet Friday deadline pressure but violates the "path from submission to export is inspectable" tenet when two workers submit at nearly the same time and arrival order is arbitrary. Including all competing revisions (e.g., suffixed rows) would preserve data but burden the partner with disambiguation work that belongs to the coordinators. |
+| Revision resolution | Coordinator selects one winning revision | Coordinator edits synthesized revision, automatic selection by submission time or author seniority | Selecting a winner preserves an actual submitted observation. Allowing coordinators to synthesize a revision (combining fields from multiple competing revisions) would provide more flexibility but loses the "this is what a worker observed" property and requires a synthesis UI. Automatic selection by timestamp treats arrival order as authority, which the problem statement explicitly rejects. Automatic selection by author seniority would require an authority hierarchy the team does not have. |
